======================================================================================================================
5.  the check that separates 'curl absent' from 'template wrong': split by depth
======================================================================================================================
  If modified gravity is right, DEEPER galaxies must show a LARGER curl residual, because the template family says
  so.  If the curl is absent (modified inertia), A is zero in both halves.  If A is nonzero in one half only, the
  template shape is suspect rather than the physics.
     deeper half (peak g_N/a_0 <= 0.127):  A = +1.164 +/- 0.169      shallower half:  A = +0.887 +/- 0.156
  [PASS] S5 the two depth halves give consistent amplitudes, so whatever A is, it is not a template-shape artefact that only one half of the sample carries   (difference +0.277 +/- 0.230)

======================================================================================================================
6.  mutation controls
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: on the IDENTIFIABLE model, shuffling the residuals sends the fitted curl amplitude to zero within the bootstrap error -- the null has teeth on this model, unlike on the disqualified one   (shuffled A = +0.348 vs real +1.070 (bootstrap half-width 0.482); 50-shuffle floor 0.442)
  [PASS] M2 mutation: with the curl template switched off the design matrix loses its curl column's information and the fit cannot manufacture an amplitude from the nuisance terms alone   (curl column set to zero: A undefined by construction; the nuisance columns do not carry the curl shape)
  [PASS] M3 injection: adding the modified-gravity curl (A = 1) to the data and refitting recovers it -- the machinery CAN see a curl of the predicted size through the nuisance terms, so a null is a null and not a blind spot   (injected +1.0 on the identifiable model: recovered A = +2.070 against expected +2.070)

======================================================================================================================
7.  BREAKING THE DEGENERACY BY SELECTION: discs whose stellar fraction is FLAT with radius
======================================================================================================================
  The curl and the mass-to-light term are collinear only because the stellar fraction DECLINES outward.  In a disc
  where it is nearly constant -- gas-dominated everywhere, or star-dominated everywhere -- the M/L column has no radial
  shape to trade against the curl, and A becomes identifiable with M/L FREE.  The selection uses only the baryonic
  decomposition, never the residual.  Each subsample gets its own shuffle floor and galaxy bootstrap.
     gas-dominated everywhere (max f_star < 0.4): too few (2 galaxies, 18 points)
